= Related Work <sec:related_work>

This section reviews the scientific literature at the intersection of software engineering economics in the pre-project phase and the emergence of agents based on Large Language Models (LLMs), synthesizing the methodological gap motivating this work.

== Pre-Project Decision Frameworks and Intangible Asset Evaluation <sec:related_work:preproyecto>

The _Software Engineering Economics_ knowledge area formalized in SWEBOK @swebok_v4 defines the _Engineering Decision-Making Process_ as a systematic lifecycle for selecting technological alternatives to maximize business value. However, this cycle remains inherently abstract: it prescribes the formulation, search, and selection stages, but fails to operationalize empirical data capture within real organizations. Pre-project methodological frameworks such as DTGEP @sanchez_DTGEP_2022 demonstrate that rigorous digital transformation planning requires articulating three dimensions: organizational maturity, underlying knowledge assets, and formal alternative prioritization.

To evaluate an organization's baseline state, the literature commonly resorts to maturity models. While established standards like CMMI @chrissis2011cmmi assess software development process maturity, frameworks such as ITIL @axelos2019itil and COBIT @isaca2019cobit target IT service management and governance. Similarly, corporate digital maturity models typically evaluate the mere adoption of visible technologies. In contrast, the ALTUS model @sanchez_ALTUS_2020 @sanchez_knowledge_2020 focuses specifically on knowledge governance, analyzing 51 organizational capabilities across four maturity levels and seven architectural tiers to diagnose which internal capabilities are consolidated before committing to software investments.

Regarding operational activity modeling, traditional frameworks such as _Business Process Management_ (BPM) @dumas2018fundamentals, _Value Stream Mapping_ (VSM) @rother1999learning, and enterprise architecture standards like TOGAF @opengroup2018togaf represent observable workflows and infrastructure. However, they remain blind to intangible capital (accumulated experience, cultural friction, and tacit knowledge). To bridge this gap, the SIPAC methodology @sanchez_sipac_2017 @sanchez_uncovering_2016 discovers and characterizes process intangible assets by evaluating their business impact and quality level. By projecting these assets onto two-dimensional matrices, they are classified into health states (_Stable_, _Evolving_, _Replaceable_, or _Warning_), allowing the deduction of mitigating technological artifacts targeted at resolving identified operational bottlenecks @sanchez_intangibles_2021.

In selecting technological investments, industry often relies on financial metrics such as Return on Investment (ROI) or Cost-Benefit Analysis. Nevertheless, these metrics exhibit severe limitations when attempting to incorporate qualitative factors, adoption risks, and strategic alignment. Although Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM) methods such as TOPSIS @opricovic_2004_topsis and PROMETHEE @brans_1986_promethee @ozakinci_2022_early have been applied in software engineering, the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) @AHP_2001 @munier_2020_ahp provides a distinctive advantage: it hierarchically decomposes conflicting objectives and derives priorities through pairwise comparisons, ensuring formal control over the evaluator's transitive consistency ($"CR" < 0.10$).

The DTGEP methodology @sanchez_DTGEP_2022 orchestrates these disciplines by sequentially integrating ALTUS (maturity diagnosis), SIPAC (intangible asset characterization and artifact deduction), and AHP (multicriteria prioritization). However, all these methodologies share a critical operational barrier: they assume that organizational data are already formalized. In pre-project practice, knowledge regarding capabilities and processes resides tacitly in the minds of stakeholders @sanchez_uncovering_2016. Manual qualitative elicitation through semi-structured interviews @swebok_v4 renders the software engineer an unsustainable operational bottleneck, causing organizations to bypass these methodologies and adopt technology without analytical justification.

== AI in Intangibles and LLM Agents in Software Engineering <sec:related_work:llms>

Prior work has explored applying Artificial Intelligence to intangible asset modeling. Recent studies combine Natural Language Processing (NLP) and process mining (the C4PM method) to discover intellectual capital from document repositories and system logs @sanchez_identifying_2025. Furthermore, simulations based on System Dynamics and Agent-Based Modeling have been proposed to study process asset behavior @sanchez_system_2018 @sanchez_biomimetic_2017. Nonetheless, these approaches share a common limitation: they are passive, _post-hoc_ analytical methodologies dependent on pre-existing digital records. In early pre-project phases, tacit knowledge is not recorded in any repository, requiring proactive conversational elicitation directly from human actors.

Concurrently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have disrupted software engineering. A systematic review of 395 primary studies confirms their application across more than 85 software tasks, including code generation, defect detection, and unit testing @hou_2024_llm4se. In interactive settings, the _ReAct_ (_Reasoning and Acting_) paradigm @yao_react_2023 enables models to interleave reasoning with tool execution. Recent proposals such as LLMREI @korn_2025_llmrei and multi-agent frameworks like MedNegotiator @tanhaei_2026_mednegotiator demonstrate that autonomous agents can conduct requirements elicitation interviews with human-level competence. However, these advances are strictly confined to technical requirements engineering for software applications (user stories, functional specifications), without addressing holistic organizational maturity assessment or pre-project intangible asset auditing.

== Limitations of Direct Prompting and Gap Synthesis <sec:related_work:brecha>

Faced with the temptation to replace this methodological framework with direct queries to an LLM (a single prompt requesting an end-to-end digitalization plan), the literature reports critical shortcomings for organizational decision-making. First, LLMs act as statistical manipulators of linguistic form without access to underlying meaning @bender_2021_parrots, maintaining a persistent risk of hallucinations and ungrounded fabrications regardless of model scale @ji_2023_hallucination. Second, their self-generated reasoning chains often represent _post-hoc_ rationalizations or sycophancy toward user bias rather than formally verifiable logical traces @zhao_2023_explainability. Furthermore, latent training biases induce premature prescription of commercial platforms @gallegos_2024_bias without prior evaluation of organizational functional requirements. Finally, there is an intrinsic algebraic inability: unstructured LLMs cannot guarantee mathematical decision properties, such as the reciprocal consistency of pairwise comparisons ($"CR" < 0.10$) or deterministic calibration of maturity levels.

Therefore, a dual gap emerges: classical software engineering provides formal rigor and mathematical consistency but lacks automation in qualitative data gathering; generative AI offers adaptive conversational flexibility but lacks axiomatic guarantees, auditability, and consistency. As summarized in @tbl:comparativa_enfoques, _AI-Driven DTGEP_ addresses this gap by decoupling dialectical interaction (delegated to LLM agents) from decision inference (governed by structured data contracts and deterministic algorithms).

